/*
:name: array_store_stale_rhs
:type: equiv
:verilog_top: array_store_stale_rhs
:pyrope_top: array_store_stale_rhs
:state_pair_gap: mems=0/1
*/
// TRACKER — SILENT MISCOMPILE in tolg's array-element store (lhdsuite
// array_problem.md). This moved here from tests/fixme/ (compile-only, which
// could never observe a clean-compile miscompile) so the failure could be
// LEC-gated against the golden .v; the implementation has since been fixed.
//
// `arr[0] = m` stores m's DECLARATION value (0) instead of its live value (d).
// Emitted netlist: `arr_data['sb0] = 'sb0;` — a constant zero, where it must be
// `d`. So `o` reads 0 for sel==0 regardless of `d`. The golden .v beside this
// file is the SEMANTIC mux (o = sel ? 0 : d), deliberately array-free so yosys
// reads it exactly and the v2prp2v round trip of the golden stays green.
//
// BOTH ingredients are required (each verified by a passing counter-variant):
//   * the READ index must be non-constant (`arr[sel]`). With `arr[0]` the front
//     end degenerates the array to a scalar bit-select and the bug is bypassed.
//   * the store RHS must be a `mut` DECLARED in one statement and ASSIGNED in
//     another. `mut m:u1 = d` (decl+init together) is lowered CORRECTLY, as is
//     a literal RHS (`arr[1] = 17`) and a direct port RHS (`arr[0] = d`).
// Reading the same `m` into a SCALAR output in the same def is also correct —
// so the defect is specific to the array-store `din`, not to `m`'s binding.
// The CLOCKED twin (a `reg` array fed through an intermediate) lowers
// CORRECTLY — this is a comb-array (`mut`) defect only; see the
// arr_comb_mux_const / arr_comb_tmp pairs and lhdsuite array_problem.md.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS: this is the root cause of `minion_dcache_tensor_load`
// refuting in //bench:minion_lec. There `mut _mux_33:u5 = 0` is hoisted, then
// assigned from an enum, then stored into `my_miss_id[0]` — so all four array
// entries hold 0 and `l2_req_o.id` reads 0 for any index.
pub comb array_store_stale_rhs(sel:u1, d:u1) -> (o:u1) {
  mut m:u1 = 0
  mut arr:[2]u1 = 0
  m = d
  arr[0] = m
  o = arr[sel]
}
